<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">50 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Colonia Agrippina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium was the Roman city that became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>. Founded on the site of the main settlement of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a>, it received the rank of a colony in 50 CE under Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/claudius">Claudius</a> at the request of his wife Agrippina, who had been born there. It became the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania-inferior">Germania Inferior</a> and one of the largest cities of the Roman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/colonia-claudia-ara-agrippinensium.jpg" alt="Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: VollwertBIT · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roemerturm_Koeln2007.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> wiped out the Eburones in a war of reprisal, the Roman general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcus-vipsanius-agrippa">Marcus Vipsanius Agrippa</a> resettled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a> from the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in the Cologne Lowland in 38 BCE. Their settlement, probably called Oppidum Ubiorum, stood on a flood-free rise between today's Heumarkt and Alter Markt and already had a Roman grid of streets. Under Augustus the Altar of the Ubii was built there, perhaps meant as the centre of worship for a larger Germanic province, a plan largely given up after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> in 9 CE. Germanicus had his headquarters there from 13 to 17 CE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The colony's name joined the emperor, the empress's compatriots, whom she named Agrippinenses, and the altar on the forum. From 70 CE the city had a strong wall about 8 metres high, and in 80 CE the Eifel Aqueduct began to bring it 20,000 cubic metres of water a day. In the civil war after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nero">Nero</a>'s death in 68, the legions stationed there proclaimed their commander Aulus Vitellius emperor; during the Batavian revolt the townspeople, still mostly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ubii">Ubii</a>, at first sided with the rebels, then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> when told to tear down their wall. The governor resided in the Praetorium, rebuilt in 184 or 185 under Didius Julianus.</p><p>In 260 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/postumus">Postumus</a> made the city the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallic-empire">Gallic Empire</a>, which lasted fourteen years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-i">Constantine I</a> built a bridge over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in 310, guarded by the fort of Divitia, and a letter of his of 321 documents <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in the city. In December 355 the city was abandoned after a long siege by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and lay in ruins; it fell finally to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ripuarian-franks">Ripuarian Franks</a> in 459. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> stayed inhabited within its Roman layout, with about 3,000 people around 700, and the Praetorium served as a palace of the Merovingian kings.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Roman grid survives in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>'s street plan: Hohe Straße follows the cardo maximus and Schildergasse the decumanus maximus. Remains include the northwest Roman Tower of the wall, the foundations of the Praetorium under the city hall and the late Roman core of St. Gereon's Basilica, standing up to 16.50 metres high. St. Mary in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capitoline-hill">Capitol</a> stands on the site of the Capitoline temple. The Romano-Germanic Museum keeps the gate arch inscribed CCAA, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dionysus">Dionysus</a> mosaic and a large collection of the city's Roman glass.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Colonia Claudia Ara Agrippinensium” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Fearing punishment from Constantius II after forged letters had made him appear a traitor, the general Silvanus declared himself emperor at <b>Colonia Agrippina</b> (modern Cologne).</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-11">11 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Cologne grew from the Roman colony of <b>Colonia Agrippina</b> and was ruled for centuries by its archbishops.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-cologne">Free Imperial City of Cologne</a></li></ul></section>
